","note":"The package flags unmapped_code|test/shell.d/menu-images-test.sh:stop_picker_shell (unmapped_code, severity low): New function test/shell.d/menu-images-test.sh:stop_picker_shell has no function-level trace; only its file is traced (to 4 requirement(s): SW-REQ-260922-43HQ, SW-REQ-260922-MH9B, SW-REQ-260929-REJT, SW-REQ-260929-THMB), which does not establish what this code is for. The author's position (noise) is judgement, not verified; a human decision settles it.","status":"confirmed"}}],"packageDigest":"sha256:af27b7bfcce160fc319e7b856bb8d46c7699912448eeb21dfd64a585c4115e9c","packageIdentity":"epkg-3c57d53cff5c9c26688ee3746893f3c5dac41bf564a4126435fd3fc297f949dd","riskAssessment":{"level":"medium","rationale":"A targeted fix in two halves. The tests our proof work added kill a stand-in shell after the summon for select, input and images, and replays of the real Menu.qml bodies answer every displaced caller. But changed-code coverage gives no evidence: the bash is unmeasured, and the QML guards are not counted. Two paths still behave badly: a refused summon (`unknown`, exit 0) still waits, and an unscoped pgrep can abandon a live picker on a multi-account machine. 15 unapproved compat changes keep readiness not_assessed until a reviewer decides. Advisory only."},"schemaVersion":"reqproof.review-annotation.v1","suggestedTests":[{"forClaim":26,"rationale":"In menu-dmenu-test.sh, add a FAKE_ANSWER that echoes unknown with exit 0 while the stand-in shell stays alive. Assert omarchy-menu-select and -input exit 1 promptly. This fails on the current head and passes with a reply check like images:449.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-dmenu-test.sh","kind":"file","line":70},{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-select","kind":"file","line":98}],"title":"Refused summon: stub omarchy-shell prints `unknown` and exits 0"},{"forClaim":27,"rationale":"Stub pgrep to list an unsignalable pid whose cmdline matches before the caller's own stand-in. Assert the picker waits for its answer instead of exiting 1 after about a second. Running the stub as a second user would make the test exact.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-handshake","kind":"file","line":26}],"title":"Pid lookup ignores another account's shell"},{"forClaim":28,"rationale":"Make the stub omarchy-shell kill the stand-in and start a new one before it accepts the summon, then answer from the new one. Assert what the maintainers want: the answer arrives, or a clear failure.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-select","kind":"file","line":93}],"title":"Shell replaced between pid lookup and summon"},{"forClaim":29,"rationale":"On a live session: open a picker, then disable omarchy.menu (or trigger rescanPlugins). Record whether the caller exits. That measures the PR's stated remaining edge.","refs":[{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":75}],"title":"Plugin teardown with a pending request"},{"rationale":"Only interleave-02 exercises openExistingMenu's guard in the behaviour diff (the menu-dmenu-test.sh node test covers it as a unit). A short dedicated .events input would pin the third commit in the differential too. In my replay it is STRANDED at base and <cancel> at the head.","refs":[{"id":"shell/plugins/menu/Menu.qml","kind":"file","line":926},{"id":"test/bdiff/harness.mjs","kind":"test"}],"title":"Behaviour-diff corpus: select, then a regular menu, then Escape (S M C)"}],"summary":"**What the PR does.** Upstream #9031 (markvogel) fixes menu callers that wait forever. Commit b5ee064d adds bin/omarchy-menu-handshake. omarchy-menu-select, -input and -images record the pid of the quickshell serving $OMARCHY_PATH, check the summon exit status, and stop waiting about a second after that process is gone. Commits bc8f540d and aa732d0f make Menu.qml's openDmenu and openExistingMenu answer a request they displace, writing its done file alone through a new releaseProc queue (finishDoneFile). On the mirror that is c88f3f07. Every later commit is our proof work: a peer-death reproducer, stand-in-shell tests, witnesses and traces, the #9057 report flipped to fixed, and a behaviour-diff harness that can replay the new helpers.\n\n**Is the fix verified?** Not by coverage. The bash files were not measured (coverage_unmeasured) and Menu.qml's changes count 0 decisions, so \"0 uncovered\" says nothing. Test evidence: 68 results ingested, 68 passed, 0 failed. The external_call_timeout_bounded cells of Q6ZS, 9ABD, MP00 and MH9B are observed_pass from stand-in-shell tests in which the shell dies before answering. A node test runs the real openDmenu/openExistingMenu bodies (ZJEY error_handling: observed_pass). These tests are ours, not the PR's.\n\n**Compatibility.** 15 compat findings, all unapproved: 14 differential, 1 test_diff. Each prevents readiness. Readiness is not_assessed. The next decision is human_judgement (compat_changes_unapproved), by a reviewer. Nothing in this review is an approval. In every one of the 14 differences, callers the base leaves STRANDED are answered `<cancel>` at the head. I re-ran the harness on the 10 inputs behind them, and no other line differs.\n\n**Questions for a human.** (1) A summon the shell refuses with `unknown` exits 0, so its caller still waits. (2) Without -u, pgrep can return another account's shell. (3) A restart between the pid lookup and the summon. (4) Menu teardown with a request pending (the PR names this edge).\n\n**Traceability.** Our proof work adds SW-REQ-261006-ZJEY (a displaced request is answered as cancelled; still in review). It, Q6ZS, 9ABD and MH9B match at function level. 4079, EFNR and FGZQ match only because the new releaseProc's `onExited` shares a name with their handlers. The openDmenu/openExistingMenu edits map only at file level. Change effects were not analyzed.\n\n**Not caused by this PR.** No check failed. A pick made while resultProc is still writing is still lost (KI-MENU-REQUEST-LIFECYCLE)."}
